# Line endings are part of this repository's test contract, so they are pinned
# here rather than left to each machine's Git configuration.
#
# Without this file, Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` checks every
# text file out as CRLF. Three things then break, none of them obviously about
# line endings:
#
#   * `.expected` goldens arrive as CRLF while a program's output is LF, so every
#     golden comparison fails until the harness normalises both sides - which the
#     shell suites do not, leaving them unusable on Windows.
#   * A parse error quotes the offending source, and a CRLF source file puts the
#     carriage return inside the quoted text: `String(" >\r\n>> ")` on Windows
#     against `String(" >\n>> ")` on Linux. Identical sources, different
#     diagnostics.
#   * `.input` fixtures feed the interpreter through stdin, where a stray CR is a
#     character the program under test was never meant to see.
#
# `eol=lf` keeps the working tree the same on all three platforms, which is the
# only way the same test can assert the same bytes everywhere.
#
# Applying this to an existing clone needs one command, because Git does not
# rewrite files already on disk:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells read these through the legacy console and want CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Not text: Git must never touch a byte.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.zyc  binary
*.msi  binary
*.deb  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
